AuraKey SysVinit & LightDM Integration

This guide outlines how to configure AuraKey under SysVinit (systemd-free environments like Devuan) to run securely before the graphical login manager (lightdm), ensuring LUKS partitions and keyfiles are successfully decrypted before the desktop environment starts.
1. Required LSB Header Adjustments

To prevent dependency loops (insserv errors) while ensuring LightDM waits for AuraKey to finish, update the LSB headers in both initialization scripts.
AuraKey Init Script (/etc/init.d/aurakey)

Ensure AuraKey requests local filesystems and declares itself as an interactive service:
### BEGIN INIT INFO
# Provides:          aurakey
# Required-Start:    $local_fs $remote_fs
# Required-Stop:     $all
# Should-Start:      
# Should-Stop:
# Default-Start:     2 3 4 5
# Default-Stop:      0 1 6
# Short-Description: AuraKey Drivecrypt Service
# X-Interactive:     true
### END INIT INFO

LightDM Init Script (/etc/init.d/lightdm)

Add aurakey to Should-Start so LightDM delays starting until AuraKey has executed:
### BEGIN INIT INFO
# Provides:          lightdm
# Required-Start:    $local_fs $remote_fs $syslog
# Should-Start:      $all aurakey
# Required-Stop:     
# Default-Start:     2 3 4 5
# Default-Stop:      0 1 6
# Short-Description: Light Display Manager
### END INIT INFO

2. Updating Boot Dependencies

After modifying the LSB headers, refresh the SysVinit runlevel links:
sudo update-rc.d -f aurakey remove
sudo update-rc.d -f lightdm remove

sudo update-rc.d aurakey defaults
sudo update-rc.d lightdm defaults

3. Key Operational Notes

Absolute Paths: Always resolve paths relative to the script directory inside aurakey.sh (e.g., SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)"), because SysVinit executes boot scripts with / as the working directory.

Plymouth Handling: If Plymouth is active, AuraKey triggers plymouth ask-for-password natively; otherwise, it falls back to standard text console prompts.
